/**

 @page page_exahype_multicore Tasking (multicore parallelisation)


 ExaHyPE starts from @ref page_exahype_domain-decomposition "plain domain decomposition".
 Its domain decomposition splits up the domain over multiple MPI ranks, but it also
 can make one rank hold multiple subpartitions, and these subpartitions are then
 processed by different threads.
 On top of this domain decomposition, you can add tasking which only works per rank.
 That is, if you give each rank only one subdomain and if you add tasking on top,
 then you can get genuine task parallelism.
 Typically, this is a poor idea, i.e. you are better off with a mixture of domain
 decomposition and tasking to exploit many threads.
 Further to that, only some ExaHyPE solver variants support tasking and
 therefore benefit from the discussion below.


 If you have a solver with tasking and want to activate this tasking, you have
 to do the following steps:

 1. @ref page_installation_home "Configure" Peano with multithreading support.
 2. Pick a proper @ref page_exahype_domain-decomposition "load balancing scheme" no matter if you want subpartitions per
    rank or not.
 3. Run some @ref page_exahype_runtime_analysis "performance analysis".
 4. Study the @ref page_exahype_performance_optimisation "optimisation recommendations".



 ## Using ExaHyPE 2 with tasks

 The setup instructions for tasking support are collected on
 @ref page_tarch_multicore "the multicore programming page".


 ## How the tasking works

 The solvers in ExaHyPE that support tasking work directly with the
 abstractions of the multithreading as it is provided by the
 @ref page_tarch_multicore "technical architecture". All the documentation
 how tasking works in Peano can be found there.


 ## Adding tasks to your ExaHyPE solver (programmer view)

 If you write a solver variant that adds its own tasks, you work directly with
 the abstractions from the @ref page_tarch_multicore "tarch::multicore layer".
 Each task is modelled as a subclass of tarch::multicore::Task (a functor-based
 specialisation exists if you prefer to work with lambdas). You submit a task
 through tarch::multicore::spawnTask(), optionally passing incoming and outgoing
 dependencies so that the runtime can build up the task graph. A set of tasks
 that has to run in a fork-join fashion is handed to tarch::multicore::spawnTasks()
 instead.

 */
